    "examples": [
      {
        "question_text": "(a) A class makes a cube frame representing $1\\text{ m}^3$. How long must every edge be?\n(b) Choose the better unit, $\\text{cm}^3$ or $\\text{m}^3$, for a matchbox and a storeroom.",
        "solution_text": "**Identify the space or unit being described.**\nIdentify one unit cube and two spaces of different sizes.\n\n**Use the cubic-metre unit or compare the sizes.**\n(a) Every edge is $1$ m. (b) Matchbox: $\\text{cm}^3$; storeroom: $\\text{m}^3$.\n\n**Record the answer and explain.**\nThe unit cube has one-metre edges. Centimetre-sized cubes suit the small box; metre-sized cubes suit the larger room."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "(a) A rectangular room is about $6$ m long, $4$ m wide and $3$ m high. Use cubic-metre units to estimate its volume.\n(b) A rectangular shed is about $4$ m long, $3$ m wide and $2$ m high. Is a label of $25\\text{ m}^3$ a reasonable estimate?",
        "solution_text": "**Identify the space or unit being described.**\nEstimate cubes per layer and the number of layers.\n\n**Use the cubic-metre unit or compare the sizes.**\n(a) $6\\times4=24$ cubes in one layer.\n$24\\times3=72\\text{ m}^3$.\n(b) $4\\times3=12$ cubes in one layer.\n$12\\times2=24\\text{ m}^3$.\n\n**Record the answer and explain.**\n(a) About $72\\text{ m}^3$. (b) Yes; the estimate $24\\text{ m}^3$ is close to $25\\text{ m}^3$."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "(a) A student gives a room’s volume as $36\\text{ m}^2$. Explain the unit error and what else must be included when estimating volume.\n(b) Explain what a room volume of $83\\text{ m}^3$ means.",
        "solution_text": "**Identify the space or unit being described.**\nDistinguish a two-dimensional area from three-dimensional space.\n\n**Use the cubic-metre unit or compare the sizes.**\n(a) $\\text{m}^2$ measures area. Include height along with length and width.\n(b) Compare the room with cubes having one-metre edges.\n\n**Record the answer and explain.**\n(a) Estimate the volume in cubic metres using cubes per layer and layers for a rectangular room. (b) It has the same volume as $83$ one-metre cubes."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "A class uses a cubic-metre frame to estimate the volume of a rectangular storage space. About five frame widths fit along its length, three along its width and two along its height. Explain how the frame helps and estimate the volume.",
        "solution_text": "The frame represents a cube with every edge $1$ m long. Use its edges to estimate each dimension in metres.\n\n[tikz]\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,draw=black,text=black,line width=0.5pt,every node/.style={font=\\fontsize{10}{12}\\selectfont}]\n\\coordinate (A) at (0,0);\n\\coordinate (B) at (2,0);\n\\coordinate (C) at (2,2);\n\\coordinate (D) at (0,2);\n\\coordinate (E) at (0.7,0.6);\n\\coordinate (F) at (2.7,0.6);\n\\coordinate (G) at (2.7,2.6);\n\\coordinate (H) at (0.7,2.6);\n\\draw[dashed] (A)--(E)--(F) (E)--(H);\n\\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--cycle;\n\\draw (B)--(F)--(G)--(H)--(D) (C)--(G);\n\\path (1,-0.3) node {1 m};\n\\path (-0.35,1) node {1 m};\n\\path (0.2,2.5) node[anchor=east] {1 m};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n[/tikz]\n\nOne layer holds about $5\\times3=15$ cubic-metre units. There are about two layers.\n\n$15\\times2=30\\text{ m}^3$.\n\nThe estimated volume is $30\\text{ m}^3$."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "A rectangular walk-in cupboard is about $2$ m long, $2$ m wide and $2$ m high. Which is the more sensible volume estimate: $1\\text{ m}^3$ or $10\\text{ m}^3$? Explain.",
        "solution_text": "Compare the cupboard with cubes having one-metre edges.\n\nOne layer holds about $2\\times2=4$ cubes. About two layers fit in its height.\n\n$4\\times2=8\\text{ m}^3$.\n\n$10\\text{ m}^3$ is the more sensible estimate because it is closer to $8\\text{ m}^3$."
      }
    ],
